From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: phy: aquantia: stop the MIB thread when the PHY goes away

The MIB debugfs support added by 999-ephy-aqr113c-04-add-mib-debugfs.patch
starts a kernel thread in aqr107_probe() that dereferences the phy_device
once a second:

	static int aqr107_mib_thread(void *data)
	{
		struct phy_device *phydev = data;

		for (;;) {
			if (kthread_should_stop())
				break;
			aqr107_mib_read(phydev);
			set_current_state(TASK_INTERRUPTIBLE);
			schedule_timeout(HZ);
		}
		return 0;
	}

Nothing ever calls kthread_stop(): the driver has no .remove handler. When
the PHY is torn down the thread keeps running and touches freed memory on
its next pass, which panics the machine. Reproduced reliably on a BPI-R4
(MT7988A, 6.12.103) by unplugging a genuine RollBall SFP+ module
(Aquantia AQR113C) from a running cage:

  Comm: aqr107_mib_thre
  Unable to handle kernel paging request at virtual address 000018421200007c
  Call trace:
    __mutex_lock.constprop.0+0x110/0x4a0
    mutex_lock+0x34/0x40
    phy_read_mmd+0x28/0x60
    aqr107_mib_read+0x24/0x2a4
    aqr107_mib_thread+0x28/0x54
    kthread+0xd8/0xdc
  Kernel panic - not syncing: Oops: Fatal exception

Removing an RTL8261BE module from the same cage is always clean, because
that module has no bridge and therefore no PHY and no MIB thread.

The module used is the BPI-SFP-10G-T, sold by Banana Pi as the official SFP+
accessory for the BPI-R4 and BPI-R4 Pro, so this is the vendor's own
recommended combination rather than an exotic part. Its EEPROM reports
vendor "OEM", part "SFP-10G-T", rev 1, sn 2606240025, with an Aquantia
AQR113C inside. Note that a second module sold under the same "OEM" /
"SFP-10G-T" strings (rev A, sn 2511140049) carries an RTL8261BE and no
bridge at all, so the EEPROM identity alone does not tell the two apart.

Add the missing .remove handler and stop the thread there. Two related
leaks in the same code path are fixed as well:

  - the debugfs file keeps phydev as its private data and outlived the
    PHY; remember its dentry and remove it,
  - debugfs_lookup() returns a dentry with an elevated refcount which was
    never dropped, leaking a reference on every probe.

Applies on top of 999-ephy-aqr113c-04-add-mib-debugfs.patch. Named "zz" so it
is applied after 999-ephy-cux3410-01-add-cux3410-support.patch, which adds one
more driver using aqr107_probe(); all 14 of them need the .remove handler.

Signed-off-by: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/phy/aquantia/aquantia.h	2026-08-29 18:58:21.463143508 +0200
+++ b/drivers/net/phy/aquantia/aquantia.h	2026-08-29 18:58:21.481895594 +0200
@@ -193,6 +193,7 @@
 	unsigned long leds_active_high;
 	struct aqr107_mib_stat mib;
 	struct task_struct *mib_thread;
+	struct dentry *mib_file;
 };
 
 #if IS_REACHABLE(CONFIG_HWMON)
@@ -202,6 +203,7 @@
 #endif
 
 int aqr_mib_probe(struct phy_device *phydev);
+void aqr_mib_remove(struct phy_device *phydev);
 
 int aqr_firmware_load(struct phy_device *phydev);
 
--- a/drivers/net/phy/aquantia/aquantia_mib.c	2026-08-29 18:58:21.465758296 +0200
+++ b/drivers/net/phy/aquantia/aquantia_mib.c	2026-08-29 18:58:21.481937080 +0200
@@ -135,6 +135,7 @@
 	};
 
 	struct aqr107_priv *priv = phydev->priv;
+	bool created = false;
 	struct dentry *root;
 	char dirname[5];
 
@@ -145,9 +146,17 @@
 		root = debugfs_create_dir("aquantia", NULL);
 		if (!root)
 			return -ENOMEM;
+		created = true;
 	}
 
-	debugfs_create_file(dirname, S_IRUGO, root, phydev, &fops_mib);
+	priv->mib_file = debugfs_create_file(dirname, S_IRUGO, root, phydev,
+					     &fops_mib);
+
+	/* debugfs_lookup() returns a dentry with an elevated refcount. The
+	 * directory is shared between PHYs and outlives us, so drop it again.
+	 */
+	if (!created)
+		dput(root);
 
 	if (!priv->mib_thread) {
 		/* create a thread for recording packet counts */
@@ -165,3 +174,25 @@
 
 	return 0;
 }
+
+void aqr_mib_remove(struct phy_device *phydev)
+{
+	struct aqr107_priv *priv = phydev->priv;
+
+	if (!priv)
+		return;
+
+	/* The MIB thread dereferences phydev once a second. It has to be gone
+	 * before the PHY is torn down, otherwise removing the module - for
+	 * example unplugging an SFP module from its cage - leaves the thread
+	 * reading freed memory, which panics the machine.
+	 */
+	if (priv->mib_thread) {
+		kthread_stop(priv->mib_thread);
+		priv->mib_thread = NULL;
+	}
+
+	/* The debugfs file keeps phydev as its private data; drop it too. */
+	debugfs_remove(priv->mib_file);
+	priv->mib_file = NULL;
+}
--- a/drivers/net/phy/aquantia/aquantia_main.c	2026-08-29 18:58:21.467916672 +0200
+++ b/drivers/net/phy/aquantia/aquantia_main.c	2026-08-29 18:58:21.482109918 +0200
@@ -950,6 +950,11 @@
 	return err;
 }
 
+static void aqr107_remove(struct phy_device *phydev)
+{
+	aqr_mib_remove(phydev);
+}
